<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Karabakh Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1822 CE · capital Shusha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate founded by Panah Ali Khan in Karabakh, whose fortress capital Shusha withstood Agha Muhammad Khan Qajar and became a cultural centre.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> death in 1747, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panah-ali-khan">Panah Ali Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanshir">Javanshir</a> tribe returned from exile and made himself khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>. He built a series of fortresses and in the 1750s founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> on a mountain plateau, while forcing the Armenian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melikdoms-of-karabakh">Melikdoms of Karabakh</a> to accept his authority.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate ruled over Turkic tribal groups in the lowlands and Armenian villages in the highlands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a>, from 1763, was a powerful ruler, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molla-panah-vagif">Molla Panah Vagif</a>, shaped court culture. In 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> withstood a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Muhammad Khan Qajar</a>, who in 1797 took the town but was murdered there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kurekchay">Treaty of Kurekchay</a> accepting Russian suzerainty. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdi-qoli-khan">Mehdi Qoli Khan</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 1822, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished the khanate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karabakh-khanate.jpg" alt="Karabakh Khanate" width="320" height="259"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Karabakh_Khanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shusha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Panah Ali Khan, Ibrahim Khalil Khan, Mehdi Qoli Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam; Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani; Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Karabakh, Qarabagh Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1785&amp;focus=karabakh-khanate" aria-label="Karabakh Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5833 754 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Karabakh Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6153 1010L6298 1144M6153 1010L6298 1144M6153 1010L6487 457M6153 1010L6364 1269"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6298 1144h0M6487 457h0M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shusha. <a href="/atlas?year=1785&amp;focus=karabakh-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
